1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,350
You like living in New York, don't you?

2
00:00:02,350 --> 00:00:03,420
Oh, I love it.

3
00:00:03,420 --> 00:00:04,860
It's so convenient.

4
00:00:04,860 --> 00:00:06,280
I can take the bus to work ...

5
00:00:06,280 --> 00:00:07,090
or the subway ...

6
00:00:07,090 --> 00:00:08,250
or a taxi.

7
00:00:08,250 --> 00:00:10,430
And there's so much to do.

8
00:00:10,430 --> 00:00:13,400
Lots of movie houses, and the theater.

9
00:00:13,400 --> 00:00:15,200
I know what you mean.

10
00:00:15,200 --> 00:00:16,980
I'd like to live in the city,

11
00:00:16,980 --> 00:00:18,890
but living in New Jersey and the suburbs

12
00:00:18,890 --> 00:00:20,000
is better for Michelle.

13
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:22,490
Trees, grass.

14
00:00:22,490 --> 00:00:25,520
There's a lot of good things about suburban living.

15
00:00:25,520 --> 00:00:27,660
I grew up in Riverdale, remember?

16
00:00:27,660 --> 00:00:28,760
So I know.

17
00:00:28,760 --> 00:00:31,350
But, as a working woman,

18
00:00:31,350 --> 00:00:33,350
I think New York has all the conveniences--

19
00:00:33,350 --> 00:00:40,020
including the best tomatoes.

20
00:00:40,020 --> 00:00:43,070
The truth is, I'd like to live in the city.

21
00:00:43,070 --> 00:00:45,200
Michelle's the right age.

22
00:00:45,200 --> 00:00:47,700
There are lots of things for her here.

23
00:00:47,700 --> 00:00:48,850
You're right, Harry.

24
00:00:48,850 --> 00:00:50,700
Today is the perfect example.

25
00:00:50,700 --> 00:00:53,780
Michelle and her friends are at the aquarium in Brooklyn.

26
00:00:53,780 --> 00:00:55,490
They come back here for lunch,

27
00:00:55,490 --> 00:00:58,200
then go uptown to the Museum of Natural History.

28
00:00:58,200 --> 00:01:00,520
There's so much for young people to see and do.

29
00:01:00,520 --> 00:01:01,890
It's just incredible!

30
00:01:01,890 --> 00:01:03,010
Not just for young people.

31
00:01:03,010 --> 00:01:04,140
What about me?

32
00:01:04,140 --> 00:01:05,590
I've never been to the aquarium

33
00:01:05,590 --> 00:01:06,990
or the Museum of Natural History.

34
00:01:06,990 --> 00:01:07,770
Have you?

35
00:01:07,770 --> 00:01:08,850
Oh yes, Harry.

36
00:01:08,850 --> 00:01:09,830
My mother and father

37
00:01:09,830 --> 00:01:12,270
often took us somewhere in the city on the weekends.

38
00:01:12,270 --> 00:01:13,560
Dad was a busy doctor,

39
00:01:13,560 --> 00:01:15,980
but he usually managed to squeeze a Sunday in

40
00:01:15,980 --> 00:01:17,470
with Richard, Robbie, and me.

41
00:01:17,470 --> 00:01:20,810
I used to love to go to the Metropolitan Museum of Art.

42
00:01:20,810 --> 00:01:22,080
I've been there several times.

43
00:01:22,080 --> 00:01:24,490
Twice with Michelle.

44
00:01:24,490 --> 00:01:26,830
You ought to think about spending more time with Michelle

45
00:01:26,830 --> 00:01:29,520
at all the great places in the city.

46
00:01:29,520 --> 00:01:32,200
Well, perhaps,

47
00:01:32,200 --> 00:01:35,520
you'll help me select some of the great places.

48
00:01:35,520 --> 00:01:40,410
And perhaps, you'll join us?

49
00:01:40,410 --> 00:01:47,510
Perhaps I will.

50
00:01:47,510 --> 00:01:48,930
Well, there we are.

51
00:01:48,930 --> 00:01:50,550
All set for lunch, Harry.

52
00:01:50,550 --> 00:01:51,580
It looks inviting.

53
00:01:51,580 --> 00:01:53,160
I wish Michelle and her friends would get here.

54
00:01:53,160 --> 00:01:55,220
I'm starving, aren't you?

55
00:01:55,220 --> 00:01:57,860
I can't wait to take a bite of the pumpernickel.

56
00:01:57,860 --> 00:02:00,500
It smells so delicious.

57
00:02:00,500 --> 00:02:05,360
Coming! Coming!

58
00:02:05,360 --> 00:02:06,240
Who is it?

59
00:02:06,240 --> 00:02:07,980
Michelle.

60
00:02:07,980 --> 00:02:09,310
It's us, Susan.

61
00:02:09,310 --> 00:02:10,220
Come in, Michelle,

62
00:02:10,220 --> 00:02:11,440
and bring your friends along.

63
00:02:11,440 --> 00:02:12,740
We're on the fifth floor.

64
00:02:12,740 --> 00:02:18,910
Oh, you've been here before.

65
00:02:18,910 --> 00:02:21,430
Susan,

66
00:02:21,430 --> 00:02:23,900
I really appreciate

67
00:02:23,900 --> 00:02:25,790
your doing this for Michelle and her friends.

68
00:02:25,790 --> 00:02:26,840
Oh, please, Harry.

69
00:02:26,840 --> 00:02:28,830
It's nothing.

70
00:02:28,830 --> 00:02:31,540
I'm not just doing it for Michelle.

71
00:02:31,540 --> 00:02:34,800
I'm doing it for you.

72
00:02:34,800 --> 00:02:37,780
Thank you.

73
00:02:37,780 --> 00:02:43,210
I'm doing it for us, Harry.

74
00:02:43,210 --> 00:02:43,820
Well,

75
00:02:43,820 --> 00:02:47,080
it's ... it's important for Michelle to see us together more often.

76
00:02:47,080 --> 00:02:48,780
That's true.

77
00:02:48,780 --> 00:02:49,650
It's important

78
00:02:49,650 --> 00:02:53,240
for Michelle and me to get to know each other better.

79
00:02:53,240 --> 00:02:58,460
That's important for us.

80
00:02:58,460 --> 00:03:01,050
That makes me feel good.

81
00:03:01,050 --> 00:03:02,230
What, Harry?

82
00:03:02,230 --> 00:03:03,540
What makes you feel good?

83
00:03:03,540 --> 00:03:05,120
Well, that you care about Michelle,

84
00:03:05,120 --> 00:03:06,680
that you care about me, and

85
00:03:06,680 --> 00:03:11,240
that you care about us.

86
00:03:11,240 --> 00:03:14,690
Well, Harry, that's because I do.

87
00:03:14,690 --> 00:03:19,640
I do care.

88
00:03:19,640 --> 00:03:21,110
They'll be here any minute.

89
00:03:21,110 --> 00:03:28,070
Susan, I'd like to continue this conversation later.

90
00:03:28,070 --> 00:03:29,750
Of course, Harry.

91
00:03:29,750 --> 00:03:33,780
We'll finish the conversation when they go to the museum.

92
00:03:33,780 --> 00:03:35,890
I'd like that.

93
00:03:35,890 --> 00:03:38,890
So would I.
